{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Cart line
  Phase 8. One line of the cart, used by the drawer and by the cart page.

  Accepts:
  - item     {Object}  required. A Shopify line_item.
  - index    {Number}  required. forloop.index — the line's 1-based position.
  - form_id  {String}  optional. The id of the cart form, for inputs that are
                       not descendants of it.
  - scope    {String}  optional. Namespaces every id this line emits. Two
                       surfaces can render the same cart line, and an id built
                       from the line key alone collides across them — which
                       silently re-points one copy's <label for> at the other
                       copy's input.
  - compact  {Boolean} optional, default false. The drawer size.
  - eager    {Boolean} optional, default false. Load this thumbnail eagerly.
                       True only for the first line of the cart PAGE, where it
                       is the largest above-the-fold element.

  Usage:
  {% for item in cart.items %}
    {% render 'cart-line-item', item: item, index: forloop.index %}
  {% endfor %}

  THE LINE WORKS WITH JAVASCRIPT OFF.
  The quantity field is name="updates[]" — Shopify's own documented cart form
  field — and removal is item.url_to_remove, Shopify's own documented removal
  link. Submitting the surrounding form updates the cart; following the link
  removes the line. The Ajax layer intercepts both and does the same thing
  without a reload. Nothing in this file is script-only.

  updates[] IS POSITIONAL.
  The array index maps to the cart line number, so exactly one input is
  emitted per line, in cart.items order, with no gaps. A quantity field wrapped
  in a condition that can be false would shift every later line's quantity onto
  the wrong product. That is why the input is never conditional here.

  FIELD NAMES THAT LOOK RIGHT AND ARE NOT.
  line_item.product_title and line_item.variant_title do not exist in Liquid —
  they exist only in the Ajax Cart API's JSON, which is a different API and the
  usual source of this mistake. The name comes from item.product.title and the
  variant line from item.options_with_values, guarded by
  has_only_default_variant so a product with no options does not print a
  spurious "Title: Default Title" row.

  item.key, NOT item.id.
  In a cart context item.id is the variant id, and two lines can share one —
  the same variant with different properties, or split by an automatic
  discount. The key identifies the line. It is also not stable across cart
  mutations, so the script re-reads it from freshly rendered markup rather than
  caching it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign line_classes = 'cart-line'
  if compact
    assign line_classes = line_classes | append: ' cart-line--compact'
  endif

  assign id_scope = scope | default: 'Cart'
  assign qty_id = id_scope | append: '-Quantity-' | append: item.key | append: '-' | append: index

  comment
    The thumbnail's slot, which the component fixes: 76px in the drawer, 96px
    on the cart page below --bp-md and 120px from it up. A flat declaration
    under-declared the widest of the three.
  endcomment
  assign media_sizes = '(min-width: 768px) 120px, 96px'
  if compact
    assign media_sizes = '76px'
  endif

  assign media_loading = 'lazy'
  if eager
    assign media_loading = 'eager'
  endif
  assign has_discount = false
  if item.original_line_price > item.final_line_price
    assign has_discount = true
  endif
-%}

<li class="{{ line_classes }}" data-cart-line data-line-key="{{ item.key | escape }}" data-line-index="{{ index }}">
  <div class="cart-line__media">
    {%- if item.image != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The image is inside the product link with the title, so it carries no
        alt of its own — repeating the product name would make the link
        announce it twice. This is the same rule the Phase 6 product card uses.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="cart-line__media-link" href="{{ item.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
        {{
          item.image
          | image_url: width: 300
          | image_tag:
            class: 'cart-line__image',
            widths: '76, 96, 120, 152, 192, 240, 300',
            sizes: media_sizes,
            loading: media_loading,
            decoding: 'async',
            alt: ''
        }}
      </a>
    {%- else -%}
      <span class="cart-line__image cart-line__image--empty" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="cart-line__detail">
    <a class="cart-line__title" href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.product.title }}</a>

    {%- unless item.product.has_only_default_variant -%}
      <p class="cart-line__variant">
        {%- for option in item.options_with_values -%}
          <span class="cart-line__variant-part">
            <span class="visually-hidden">{{ option.name }}: </span>{{ option.value }}
          </span>
          {%- unless forloop.last %}<span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>{% endunless -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </p>
    {%- endunless -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Line properties a merchant or app has attached. Shopify's convention is
      that a property whose name begins with an underscore is private, so it is
      not shown, and an empty value means nothing was chosen.

      PHASE 17: BOTH HALVES ARE ESCAPED, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL.
      Shopify Liquid does not escape output. A line item property is supplied by
      whoever posted to /cart/add.js, which makes both its name and its value
      untrusted input rendered into markup. Without the filter, a property value
      of "</p><img src=x onerror=...>" closed this paragraph and produced a live
      element with an event handler — verified by rendering it.

      The theme already escapes search.terms for exactly this reason
      (sections/main-search.liquid); this was the inconsistency, not the policy.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if item.properties != blank -%}
      {%- for property in item.properties -%}
        {%- assign first_char = property.first | slice: 0 -%}
        {%- if property.last != blank and first_char != '_' -%}
          <p class="cart-line__property">{{ property.first | escape }}: {{ property.last | escape }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Discounts Shopify has applied to this line. Rendered from the platform's
      own allocations; the theme creates no discount logic of its own.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if item.line_level_discount_allocations.size > 0 -%}
      <ul class="cart-line__discounts" role="list">
        {%- for allocation in item.line_level_discount_allocations -%}
          <li class="cart-line__discount">
            {{ allocation.discount_application.title }}
            (&minus;{{ allocation.amount | money }})
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Shopify sets error_message on a line it could not honour — most often a
      quantity that exceeded stock between adding and checking out. It is the
      platform's own text, not a string this theme writes.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if item.error_message != blank -%}
      <p class="cart-line__error" role="alert">{{ item.error_message }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="cart-line__controls">
      {% render 'quantity-selector',
        id: qty_id,
        name: 'updates[]',
        value: item.quantity,
        min: 1,
        describe: item.product.title,
        form: form_id,
        line_key: item.key,
        compact: compact
      %}

      {%- comment -%}
        The floor is one, not zero. Stepping a quantity down to nothing would
        make the minus button destructive at its last press — the brief's rule
        is "do not allow quantity below 1" — so the minus disables at one and
        removal is the explicit control beside it.
      {%- endcomment -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Removal. A text control rather than a glyph: Phase 2 §18.1 closes the
        icon set to decorative additions and contains no trash mark, and in a
        420px drawer a word is less ambiguous than a bin at 44px. The
        destination is Shopify's own item.url_to_remove, so it works with no
        script; the cart script cancels the navigation and does the same thing
        through the Ajax API.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a
        class="cart-line__remove"
        href="{{ item.url_to_remove }}"
        data-cart-remove
        data-line-key="{{ item.key | escape }}"
      >
        <span aria-hidden="true">{{ 'cart.remove' | t }}</span>
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'cart.remove_title' | t: title: item.product.title }}</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="cart-line__price">
    {%- if has_discount -%}
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.price.sale_price' | t }}</span>
      <span class="cart-line__price-final">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</span>
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.price.regular_price' | t }}</span>
      <s class="cart-line__price-original">{{ item.original_line_price | money }}</s>
    {%- else -%}
      <span class="cart-line__price-final">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</li>
